<body>
    <h1>AI-284: AI+ RabbitMQ Integration</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <p>The AI+ platform currently operates through an HTTP-based API th=
at provides asynchronous processing capabilities for media files. This desi=
gn document outlines the integration of RabbitMQ to enable event-driven pro=
cessing while maintaining the existing HTTP interface. The integration will=
 allow AI+ services to consume events from storage systems and other trigge=
rs, process media files, and publish results back through RabbitMQ exchange=
s.</p>
<p>The key challenge is transitioning from a pull-based HTTP model to a pus=
h-based event model while ensuring reliable message processing, maintaining=
 job state consistency, and providing visibility into processing status acr=
oss distributed systems.</p>
<h3 id=3D"AI-284:AI+RabbitMQIntegration-RelatedDocumentation">Related Docum=
entation</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>PixStore Integration</strong>: See <a class=3D"external-link" hr=
ef=3D"https://claude.ai/chat/referenced-doc" rel=3D"nofollow">Pixstor files=
ystem integration using RabbitMQ</a> for the complete specification of even=
ts published by the storage layer</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Vision Integration</strong>: See <a class=3D"external-link" href=
=3D"https://claude.ai/chat/referenced-doc" rel=3D"nofollow">Vision Metadata=
 Integration using RabbitMQ</a> for the metadata consumption specification<=
/p></li>
</ul>
<h2 id=3D"AI-284:AI+RabbitMQIntegration-SystemArchitecture">System Architec=
ture</h2>
<h3 id=3D"AI-284:AI+RabbitMQIntegration-HTTP-BasedProcessing">HTTP-Based Pr=
ocessing</h3>
<p>The existing AI+ system follows a traditional asynchronous HTTP pattern:=
</p>
